  {
    "episode_id": 90472,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90472.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 43,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, so my dad was an infantryman in Vietnam. He got out of college in 1967 when you could roll one deferment into another. And he'd always thought, I think, that he wanted to be a coach, wanted coach football. So he became a high school coach and teacher, realized that wasn't his calling after all after he spent a year dealing with high schoolers. And so he decided in the spring of 1968 that he didn't want to stay in a job just because it had a deferment. And so many people did back in those days and just do his duty. So he decided that he first he was going to join the Marine Corps. And then my grandfather had been the Navy in World War II and seen what Marines faced then and threatened to disown him if he joined the Marines, which I've never really figured out why that was a effective threat since he didn't really have any inheritance to disown him",
