  {
    "episode_id": 90472,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90472.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 51,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, it was tough fighting so he was in Vietnam from the summer of 69 to the summer of 70 And you know Nixon had campaigned in 1968 in part on bringing the Vietnam War to an end So I think some people thought the fighting would wind down quickly as opposed to becoming more intense and he was out the jungle for you know At least half of the time he was in Vietnam and sometimes he was out walking point for the infantry squad and So it was tough fighting. I mean it's tougher I'd say than what I personally faced in Iraq or Afghanistan I mean most most veterans look back on the previous war and think you know We've got it bad now, but at least we didn't have it like that. I'm sure those guys in Vietnam said the same thing about Korea said the same thing about World War two, but it was tough fighting he didn't you know when I was growing up he didn't Glorify you know combat, you know, he didn't spend a lot of time reminiscing about his time in the army but When timing was appropriate, he would bring up lessons or memories from it. Like I said, being in the wood line, when it's rainy, and you can smell the foliage and helicopters coming over, I'm sure that would bring back a memory of Vietnam. Or when we're getting ready to go out to Deer Camp, you go into the living room and everything is laid out on the floor in a dress-right dress fashion. I didn't appreciate it at the time, but that was a skill that he learned in the army, and he'd point out how you learned these things. the army and so forth. Now he later said when I joined the army which he very much opposed that he felt like God was punishing him for what he had done to his father in 1968.",
