  {
    "episode_id": 90472,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90472.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 57,
    "turn_text": "Well, I was very fortunate in the army that I got to do exactly what I wanted to do. You know, on the morning of September 11th, 2001, I was in law school. And I thought it was going to be a lawyer. And that day pretty changed my plans dramatically, obviously. I completed school and practiced for a couple of years to pay off my loans. But then I joined and I joined to be an infantry officer on lead platoon in combat and that's what I got to do within, you know, barely a year and a half of being in the Army. A lot of people joined to do those things and they don't have that opportunity. And I did and it was, you know, it was a very, I mean, hardest job you could have, you know, leading 40 men in combat, you know, when there's bad guys all around you trying to kill you and try to kill your men, but rewarding and inspiring and just, you know, incredibly great privilege to be able to fight for a free country alongside.",
