    "turn_text": "all 40 come back? In my platoon, they did. Not in my company of battalion, unfortunately. In my platoon, though, we came back and didn't have any serious wounds while I was there. By the time I was there in 2006, the enemy had developed tactics and techniques and procedures and fighting that usually meant you weren't going to have protracted battles. I mean, anytime you're in a firefight, it seems very protracted. But the kind of, you know, hours-long battles that you might have seen set combat outpost Keating that Clint Romeshay won the Medal of Honor for, and that he has a new book about was pretty rare and bagged at the time. The enemy knew that because of our superior firepower and mobility that they couldn't stand and fight us. So the most common kind of enemy technique was roadside bombs, IEDs, or sniper fire. So we certainly got exposed to our share of that. But fortunately, you know, they're good training and discipline and good equipment. Everybody came home alive.",
