    "turn_idx": 78,
    "turn_text": "agree with that as well. We made a lot of gains in Iraq in the years after I left after the surge. I came back to the United States from Iraq just a few weeks before George Bush lost the 2006 elections and then dumped the recommendations of the Iraq Study Group, which I was I strongly opposed, but I was also surprised that he would strike out his own, I think. In retrospect, we'll look back and say that was his finest hour. But when President Obama withdrew all troops in 2011, it was not a Jeffersonian democracy. Was it stable? Was it largely representative? Did it largely have a monopoly on force? Yes. But it was not a Jeffersonian democracy the way the United States or Great Britain is. And it's probably going to be a long time before a lot of countries around the world achieve that level of political development. And we have to have our eyes open about that. In part, one of the main arguments against going on the kind of Wilsonian nation building effort is the very plethora of opportunities you have to do so all around the world.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
