  {
    "episode_id": 90472,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90472.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 82,
    "turn_text": "And in a democracy in particular, statesmen have to be attuned to durable political support for any foreign policy. We're living at a time now, and I don't mean 2017, I mean the last 26, 27 years, in which our statesmen have struggled to do something that they've never done before in over 200 years of our history, which is to develop a peacetime strategy for global leadership. We've never had to do that before. In the founding era and in the Monroe system, which really lasted from the Monroe Doctrine up to World War I, we were able to uh, you know, coast and Great Britain's wake. Um, and then we did not have a successful strategy to say the least during the interwar period. And then after World War two, um, you know, we were able to develop a strategy for global leadership, but in a period of very heightened tensions, uh, Cold War that often termed hot. So since that, that ended, you know, we've asked our statesmen to do something, um, that I would say they haven't done the best job of achieving through multiple presidencies.",
