    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90472.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 115,
    "turn_text": "So on both of those, but in particular, the European one since most of our country is at or especially in Western Europe at similar levels of development. I think the sentiment that the president rightly tapped that was also tapped in the Brexit campaign last year was the move away from traditional trade deals that lowered tariffs and quotas or subsidy systems or so forth. into what you might call regulatory harmonization schemes. If you're a voter in Great Britain, it's understandable if you're going to reduce tariffs with countries around the world. What's less understandable is why unelected bureaucrats and Brussels get to dictate the size of your olive oil decanter on your table, to use one example that was common in the Brexit vote. I think that's the sentiment the president tapped into. It's also part of the",
