    "turn_idx": 158,
    "turn_text": "So, it's not unusual, and especially in countries where personal relationships matter a lot for them to send those kind of personal emissaries. I've had some of those people who, you know, they're not the foreign minister, they're not ambassador, come to meet me shortly after the election, usually from allied countries, because they were looking for contacts inside Donald Trump's world, because they didn't think it was going to win, they didn't develop those contacts. So that's not uncommon because, again, you have to remember something that it's easy for Americans to forget, even for Americans in Washington, in government to forget. We are the most important country in the world, every country in the world. Like the diplomat that represents every country in the world that serves in Washington is the most skilled, experienced, important diplomat they have. Our ambassador in their capital is the most important ambassador in their capital. For many of these countries, their existence depends on their relationship with the United States, which is to say in no small part their relationship with the incoming president and his administration. The meetings that Jared Kushner or Mike Flynn or any other member of the administration are not out of the ordinary. Now, I can't say what happened in those meetings.",
