  {
    "episode_id": 90472,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90472.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 217,
    "turn_text": "Now, so the common complaint you'll hear from Medicaid about people who are both on Medicaid and from doctors is that it's insurance that doesn't necessarily equal care. A lot of doctors across Arkansas will not take a new Medicaid patient or they'll take one new Medicaid patient a month. And that's in part because one of the few tools that governors have is to reduce rates of reimbursement for doctors. And it gets to a point where it's simply not economically feasible. for a doctor or other health care provider to continue seeing an open-ended number of Medicaid enrollees. I think there's better ways to try to restrain the right of growth of Medicaid without getting people into the situation where they have insurance, but they don't have care. giving the governor's flexibility to have a small copay, for instance, or small deductible, giving them the flexibility to have managed care, giving them the flexibility to use telemedicine. Those are all things that governors don't currently do that are being done in the private insurance markets, and I think we should let them do that. We should see what works.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The current system risks creating situations where individuals have insurance but lack access to actual care.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "Medicaid recipients often face difficulty accessing medical care due to limited availability of doctors willing to accept them.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Reduced reimbursement rates can make it economically unfeasible for providers to treat ongoing numbers of Medicaid patients.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Governors have the power to reduce reimbursement rates for doctors treating Medicaid patients.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Some doctors in Arkansas limit their acceptance of new Medicaid patients to one per month.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Allowing governors to implement small copays or deductibles could help manage Medicaid growth sustainably.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Managed care models are effective tools used in private insurance but unavailable to governors in Medicaid.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Telemedicine is another tool available in private insurance markets but not currently permitted for Medicaid.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
